      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://group.bmj.com/group/rights-licensing/permissions.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To establish the safety of an intranasal diamorphine (IND) spray in children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "An open-label, single-dose pharmacovigilance trial."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Emergency departments in eight UK hospitals."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Children aged 2-16\u2005years with a fracture or other trauma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Adverse events (AE) specifically related to nasal irritation, respiratory and central nervous system depression."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "226 patients received 0.1\u2005mg/kg IND. No serious or severe AEs occurred. The incidence of treatment-emergent AEs (TEAEs) was 26.5% (95% CI 20.9% to 32.8%), 93% being mild. 89% were related to treatment, all being known effects of the drug or route of administration except for three events in two patients. 20.4% (95% CI 15.3% to 26.2%) patients reported nasal irritation, all mild except one moderate and one 'unknown' severity. No respiratory depression was reported. Three AEs related to reduced Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) occurred, all mild."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "There were no safety concerns raised during the conduct of the study. In addition to expected side effects, IND can cause mild nasal irritation in a proportion of patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
            "Label": "EUROPEAN UNION DRUG REGULATING AUTHORITIES CLINICAL TRIAL NO",
            "content": "2009-014982-16."
          }
        ]
      },
